== 4. Means, Rules, and Directions

I have now only one thing more to do before I close this discourse, and that is to lay down some means, rules, or directions that may help you#super[+] on in a faithful and conscientious discharge of this great duty of closet prayer. And so, as follows.

=== a. Take Heed of an Idle and Slothful Spirit

First, as ever you#super[+] would give yourselves up to private prayer, take heed of an idle and slothful spirit. If Adam in the state of innocence had to work and tend the garden, and if after his Fall, when he was monarch of all the world, he still had to labor, why should anyone be idle or slothful? Idleness is a sin against the law of creation. God created man to labor, and so the idle person violates this law of creation; for by his idleness he casts off the authority of his Creator, Who made him for labor. Idleness contradicts the principles of our creation. Man in innocence would have been free from weariness, but not from work; he was to tend the garden by divine appointment. “Then the #smallcaps[Lord] God took the man and placed him in the Garden of Eden to cultivate and keep it” (Gen 2:15). All weariness in labor, and all vexing, tiring, and tormenting labor, came in by the Fall. “By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread” (Gen 3:19). The bread of idleness is neither sweet nor sure. “An idle soul will suffer hunger,” says Solomon (Prov 19:15). An idle life and a holy heart are very far apart. “By doing nothing,” says the heathen, “men learn to do evil things.” It is easy to slip out of an idle life into an evil and wicked life. Indeed, an idle life is evil in itself, for man was made to be active, not to be idle. The Cyclops thought man’s happiness consisted #emph[in nihil agendo], in doing nothing, but no excellent thing can be the child of idleness. Idleness is a mother-sin, a breeding-sin. It is #emph[pulvinar diaboli], the devil’s cushion, on which he sits, and the devil’s anvil, on which he forges very great and very many sins (Eph 4:28; 2 Thess 3:10, 12). Just as toads and serpents breed most in standing waters, so sin thrives most in idle persons. Idleness is what provokes the Lord to forsake men’s bodies and the devil to possess their souls.

No man has fewer means to preserve his body and more temptations to infect his soul than an idle person. Oh, shake off sloth! The sluggish Christian will be sleeping, or idling, or trifling, when he should be in his closet praying. Sloth is the wasting sickness of the soul; get it cured, or it will be your eternal bane. Of all devils, it is the idle devil that keeps men out of their closets most. Nothing gives the devil so much advantage against us as idleness. It was good counsel that Jerome gave to his friend, #emph[Facito aliquid operis, ut te semper Diabolus inveniat occupatum], “Do some work so that the devil always finds you busy,” so that when the devil comes with a temptation you may answer him that you are not at leisure.

Mr. Greenham, a famous and painstaking preacher of this nation, told how a poor soul whom the devil tempted came to him for advice on how she might resist the temptation, and he gave her this answer: “Never be idle, but be always well employed. For I have found it in my own experience: when the devil came to tempt me, I told him that I was not at leisure to listen to his temptations; and by this means I resisted all his assaults.” Idleness is the hour of temptation; and an idle person is the devil’s tennis ball, tossed by him as he pleases.

“He who labors,” said the old hermit, “is tempted by only one devil, but he who is idle is assaulted by all.” Cupid complained that he could never fasten on the Muses, because he could never find them idle. The fowler bends his bow and spreads his net for birds when they are settled, not when they are on the wing. So Satan shoots his most fiery darts at men when they are most idle and slothful. And the Sodomites found this by woeful experience (Ezek 16:49), when God rained hell out of heaven on them, both for their idleness and for those other sins of theirs to which their idleness exposed them.

It was said of Rome that during the time of their wars with Carthage and other enemies in Africa, they did not know what vice meant; but no sooner had they won the victory than they came to ruin through idleness. Idleness is a sin not only against the law of grace, but also against the light of nature. You cannot look any way without every created thing rebuking and reproaching your idleness and sloth. If you look up to the heavens, there you will find all their glorious lights constant in their motions. The sun is “like a champion rejoicing to run his course” (Ps 19:5; 104:23). The winds blow, the waters run, the earth brings forth her pleasant and delightful fruits. All the fish in the sea, the birds in the air, and the beasts in the fields and on the mountains have their movements and workings, and all of them call aloud to man not to be idle, but active.

Solomon sends the sluggard to the ant to learn industry (Prov 6:6). The ant is a very little creature, but exceedingly hard-working. Nature has put an instinct into her to be very busy and active all the summer; she is at it early and late, and will not lose an hour unless the weather hinders her. And the prophet Jeremiah sends the Jews to school to learn to watch and keep their times from the stork, the turtledove, the crane, and the swallow (Jer 8:7). And our Savior sends us to the sparrows and lilies to learn to depend on providence (Matt 6:26, 28). And let me send you to the busy bee to learn activity and industry. Though the bee is little in size, yet it is great in service. She flies far, searches the fields, hedges, trees, orchards, and gardens, and loads herself with honey and wax, and then returns to her hive.

Now how the activity of these creatures should make the idle person blush! Oh, sirs! Man is the noblest creature, into whom God has put principles of the greatest activity, as being capable of the greatest and highest enjoyments. And therefore idleness is a forgetting of man’s dignity, a forsaking of the rank in which God has set him, and a debasing of himself below the least and lowest creatures, which constantly, in their order, obediently serve the law of their creation. Indeed, if you#super[+] look up to the blessed angels above you, you will always find them active and serviceable. “Are they not all ministering spirits sent to serve those who will inherit salvation?” (Heb 1:14). And if you#super[+] look down to the angels of darkness below you, oh, how laborious and industrious they are to destroy and damn your precious and immortal souls (1 Pet 5:8)!

In closing, remember that idleness is so great an evil that it has been condemned and severely punished by the very worst of men. Among the Egyptians, idleness was a capital crime. Among the Lucans, he who lent money to an idle person was to lose it. By Solon’s law, idle persons were to suffer death. And Seneca would rather be sick than idle. The Lacedaemonians called men to account for their idle hours. Among the Corinthians, idle persons were handed over to the #emph[carnifex], the executioner.

Antoninus Pius, when he was emperor, had the roofs and coverings taken off all such houses as were known to take in idle people. He declared that nothing was more unseemly, or more absurd to be allowed, than for such idle caterpillars and slow-worms to have their food and nourishment from a commonwealth to whose maintenance their industry and labor added nothing. All of this should steel us and arm us against sloth and idleness. I have dwelt the longer on this because there is no greater hindrance to closet prayer than sloth and idleness. Slothful and idle persons commonly lie in bed so long, and spend so much precious time between the comb and the mirror, and in eating, drinking, sporting, and trifling, etc., that they can find no time for private prayer. Certainly, those who would rather go sleeping to hell than sweating to heaven will never care much for closet prayer. And therefore shun sloth and idleness as you#super[+] would shun a lion in the way, poison in your food, or coals in your bosom, or else you will never find time to wait on God in your closets.

=== b. Take Heed of Spending Your Precious Time on Side Issues

Secondly, take heed of spending too much of your#super[+] precious time on side issues, on the little things of religion, such as “mint, dill, and cumin” (Matt 23:23); or in searching into the circumstances of worship, or in standing stoutly for this or that ceremony, and meanwhile neglecting the study of the covenant of grace; or in inquiring what fruit it was that Adam ate in Paradise, or in inquiring after the authors of such and such books, whose names God in His infinite wisdom has concealed, or in inquiring what God did before the world was made. When someone asked Augustine that question, he answered that God was preparing hell for such busy questioners as he was. It was a saying of Luther, “From a vainglorious teacher, from a contentious pastor, and from unprofitable questions, the good Lord deliver His church.”

It is one of Satan’s great designs to hinder men in the great and weighty duties of religion by keeping them busiest about the lowest and least matters of religion. Satan is never better pleased than when he sees Christians puzzled and perplexed about things in religion that are of no great moment or importance (Col 2:21). Those who deal and trade in religion more for a good name than for a good life, more for a good report than for a good conscience, more to please others than to honor God, etc., will take no pleasure in closet duties. Those who are busier about ceremonies than about substance, about the form of godliness than about the power (2 Tim 3:5), will never make it their business to be much with God in their closets, as is evident in the scribes and Pharisees (Matt 6:1–6). Those who are more taken up with the outward dress and style of religion than with the spirit, power, and life of religion will never care to drive a secret trade heavenward (Luke 11:34–40). There cannot be a surer or a greater mark of a hypocrite than to make a great stir about little things in religion, and meanwhile to neglect the great and main things in religion. Such people as these have all along in Scripture shown an aloofness and a complete carelessness toward closet duties. I never knew any man hot and zealous about side issues, about the little things of religion, who was ever famous for closet prayer.

=== c. Take Heed of Curiosity in Searching Dark and Abstruse Truths

Thirdly, take heed of prying curiosity, and of spending too much of your#super[+] precious time in searching into the dark, abstruse, mysterious, and hidden truths and things of God and religion that lie furthest from the understanding of the best and wisest of men. Curiosity is the spiritual adultery of the soul. Curiosity is a spiritual drunkenness. For just as the drunkard is never satisfied unless he sees the bottom of the cup, however deep it is, so those who are troubled with the itch of curiosity will say they can never be satisfied until they come to the bottom of the deepest and most profound things of God. They love to pry into God’s secrets, and to examine the mysteries of religion by their weak, shallow reason, and to be wise above what is written. Curious searchers into the deep, mysterious things of God will make all God’s depths into shallows rather than be thought unable to fathom them by the short line of their own reason.

Oh, that men would once learn to be contentedly ignorant where God would not have them know! Oh, that men were once so humble as to count it no disgrace to acknowledge some depths in God and in the blessed Scripture which their shallow reason cannot fathom! They are only a company of utter fools who aim to know more than God would have them know. Did not Adam’s tree of knowledge make him and his descendants sheer fools? He who goes to school to his own reason has a fool for his schoolmaster. The quickest way to go stark blind is to be always prying and gazing at the body of the sun; so the quickest way to spiritual blindness is to be always prying into the most secret and hidden things of God (Deut 29:29). Are there not many who, by prying long into the secrets of nature, have become archenemies of the grace of God (Rom 9:20)? Oh, that we were wise enough to wonder at the deep mysteries which we cannot understand, and to adore the depths and counsels which we cannot reach (Rom 11:33)!

Oh, let us check our curiosity in the things of God, and sit down satisfied and content to trace many of God’s actions back to hidden causes, which lie secret in the abyss of His eternal knowledge and infallible will. Christ, when He was on earth, very frequently, severely, and sharply condemned curious inquirers, as is evident from these scriptures (John 21:22; Acts 1:6–7; Luke 13:23–24). And the great reason our Savior so frequently checked this habit of curiosity was that the great indulgers of it were too often neglecters of the greater, necessary, and important points of religion.

Curiosity is one of Satan’s most dangerous devices, by which he keeps many souls out of their closets, indeed, out of heaven. When many a poor soul begins in good earnest to look toward heaven and to apply himself to closet duties, then Satan begins to stir himself, and to labor with all his might to keep the poor soul so busy with vain inquiries, curious speculations, and unprofitable curiosities that the soul has no time for closet prayer. Ah! How well it might have gone with many a man, had he spent in closet prayer only a quarter of the time that he has spent in curious inquiries after things that have not been essential to his happiness!

The heathen priests aimed at curious subtleties; they had their mythologies and strange jargon about their imaginary, inaccessible deities, to astonish and bewilder their blind, superstitious followers, and so to hold up their popish and apish idolatries in greater veneration. Oh, that there were none of this heathenish spirit among many in these days who have their faces toward heaven! Ah! How many there are who busy themselves more in searching out the reasons why man’s Fall could not be recovered than they do in recovering themselves out of their fallen state! Ah, how many there are who busy themselves more about the apostasy of the angels than they do about making sure of their share in Christ! And what a great deal of precious time some have spent in finding out the natures, distinctions, properties, and orders of angels!

That high-soaring, counterfeit Dionysius describes the hierarchy of angels as exactly as if he had lived among them. He says there are nine orders of them, which he grounds on nine words found partly in the Old Testament and partly in the New: seraphim, cherubim, thrones, powers, hosts, dominions, principalities, archangels, and angels. And he describes at length their separate natures, distinctions, and properties: that the first three orders are for immediate attendance on the Almighty, the next three orders for the general government of created things, and the last three orders for the particular good of God’s elect; that the archangel surpasses the beauty of the angels ten times, the principalities surpass the archangels twenty times, and the powers surpass the principalities forty times, etc. How he came by this learning is not known, and yet this hierarchy in these nine separate orders has passed as genuine through many ages of the church.

The Platonists were the first who divided the angels into three orders: some above heaven, called #emph[supercoelestes]\; others in heaven, called #emph[coelestes]\; and others under heaven, called #emph[subcoelestes]; and they assign them various offices accordingly. As follows,

First, those above heaven (I mean this visible heaven) continually stand before God, as they say, praising, lauding, and magnifying His name.

Secondly, those in heaven are placed there to move, rule, and govern the stars.

Thirdly, of those under heaven, some rule kingdoms, others provinces, others cities, others particular men.

Various Christian writers who have written on the hierarchy of angels follow these opinions. Now, if we were to take these guesses for real truths, it would follow that the highest angels do not minister to the saints, but only and immediately to God Himself, which is expressly contrary to various scriptures, as you#super[+] may see from these, among others (Jude 9; Luke 1:19, 26; Zech 4:10; Rev 5:6; Heb 1:14). When I was preaching on the ministry of the blessed angels, I proved then in several sermons, as some of you#super[+] may remember, that all the angels in heaven were commanded and commissioned by God to be serviceable and useful to the heirs of salvation. “Are they not all ministering spirits sent to serve those who will inherit salvation?” (Heb 1:14).

The devil knows he is no loser, and the curious soul only a very little gainer, if he can only persuade him to spend most of his precious time in studying and poring over the darkest, most mysterious, and hidden things of God. He who would rather read the Revelation of John than John’s plain epistles, or Daniel’s prophecies than David’s psalms, and is busier reconciling difficult scriptures than mortifying unruly lusts, or who is set more on vain speculations than on the things that make most for edification, is not the man who is cut out for closet prayer.

Those who aim at lofty notions and obscure expressions, and are men of abstract fancies, are only a company of wise fools who will never take any delight in being with God in a corner. Had many men spent in secret prayer only half the time that they have spent in seeking after the philosopher’s stone, how happy they might have been! Oh, how holy, how happy, how heavenly, how humble, how wise, how knowing many men might have been, had they spent in closet prayer only half the time that they have spent in searching after the things that are “hard to understand” (2 Pet 3:16)!

=== d. Take Heed of Engaging in a Crowd of Worldly Affairs

Fourthly, take heed of entangling yourselves#super[+] in a crowd of worldly affairs. Many have so much to do on earth that they have no time to look up to heaven. As much earth puts out the fire, so much worldly business puts out the fire of heavenly affections. Just as the earth swallowed up Korah, Dathan, and Abiram (Num 16:32), so much worldly business swallows up so much precious time that many men have no leisure to be with God in their closets. “This business has to be done, and that business cannot be left undone, and the other necessary matter must be attended to, so that I have no leisure to step out of my shop into my closet,” says the earthly-minded man (Phil 3:19).

So a crowd of worldly affairs crowds closet prayer right out of doors. Many drive so great a trade in their shops that their private trade with heaven is laid aside altogether. Nothing has kept men from Christ and closet prayer more than the shop, the exchange, the farm, and the oxen (Luke 14:16–22). The stars which have the smallest circuit are nearest the pole; and the men who are least entangled with worldly business are commonly nearest to God, to Christ, to heaven, and so the fitter for closet prayer. It is sad when men grasp so much business that they can have no leisure for communion with God in a corner. The noise in a mill is such that it hinders all private conversation between man and man; and so a multitude of worldly affairs makes such a noise that it hinders all private fellowship between God and the soul. If a man of much business should now and then slip into his closet, yet his head and his heart will be so filled and distracted with thoughts of his work that God will have little of him but his bodily presence, or at most only bodily exercise, which profits little (1 Tim 4:8). If Christ blamed Martha for the multitude of her household tasks (Luke 10:40–42), though they were undertaken directly to serve and entertain Him, because they hindered her in the concerns of her soul, oh, how He will one day blame all those who, by running themselves into a crowd of worldly affairs, cut themselves off from all opportunities of pouring out their souls before Him in secret!

=== e. Take Heed of Secret Sins

Fifthly, take heed of secret sins. There is no greater hindrance to secret prayer in all the world than secret sins; therefore stand on your#super[+] guard, and arm yourselves with all your might against them. There is an antipathy between secret sinning and secret praying. It comes partly from guilt, which makes the soul shy of coming under God’s secret eye, and partly from the fears, doubts, disputes, and disorders that secret sins raise in the heart. Light is not more opposite to darkness, Christ to Belial, or heaven to hell, than secret prayer is to secret sins. And therefore, whatever you#super[+] do, see that you keep clear of secret sins. For that purpose consider these four things:

==== 1. God Is Privy to Our Most Secret Sins

First, that God is privy to our most secret sins. His eye is as much on secret sins as it is on open sins. “You have set our iniquities before You, our secret sins in the light of Your presence” (Ps 90:8). God has an eye on our inmost evils; He sees all that is done in the dark. “Can a man hide in secret places where I cannot see him? declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. Do I not fill the heavens and the earth? declares the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Jer 23:24). “The eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are in every place, observing the evil and the good” (Prov 15:3). To say that God does not see the most secret sins of the children of men detracts not only from His omniscience but also from His mercy; for how can God pardon sins which He does not see to be sins? There is no cloud, nor curtain, nor moment of darkness, that can stand between the eyes of God and the ways of men. “A man’s ways are before the eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord], and the #smallcaps[Lord] examines all his paths” (Prov 5:21). In this scripture Solomon speaks mainly of the ways of the adulterer, which are usually plotted with the most cunning secrecy; yet God sees all those ways. Just as no boldness can exempt the adulterer from the justice of God, so no secrecy can hide him from the eye of God. Though men labor to hide their ways from others and from themselves, yet it is only labor in vain to try to hide them from God. Men who labor to hide God from themselves can never hide themselves from God.

I have read that Paphnutius converted Thais and Ephron, two famous prostitutes, from their uncleanness with only this argument, “That God sees all things in the dark, when the doors are fast, the windows shut, and the curtains drawn.” “Nothing in all creation is hidden from God’s sight; everything is naked and laid open (anatomized) before the eyes of Him to whom we must give account” (Heb 4:13). It is an allusion to the priests under the law: when they killed a beast, all that was within the beast was laid open and naked before the priest, so that he might see what was sound and what was corrupted. Though evil is done out of the sight of all the world, yet it is naked and laid open in the sight of Him to whom we must give account.

The sins which lie closest and lurk most secretly in the heart are as obvious and odious to God as those most plainly written on a man’s forehead. God is πανόφθαλμος, “all eye,” so that He sees all the most secret turnings and windings of our hearts. Our most secret sins are as plainly seen by Him as anything can be by us at noonday. “If I say, ‘Surely the darkness will hide me, and the light become night around me’—even the darkness is not dark to You, but the night shines like the day, for darkness is as light to You” (Ps 139:11–12). Not even the thickest clouds can bar out the observation of Him Whose eyes fill heaven and earth. What is the curtain, or the darkest night, or the double lock, or the secret room, to Him Who clearly observes all things in perfect nakedness? God has an eye on the most inward intentions of the heart and the subtlest stirrings of the spirit. Those philosophers were wrong who held that the eye and care of God reached no lower than the heavens. Certainly there is not a creature, not a thought, not a thing, but lies open to the all-seeing eye of God. The Lord knows our secret sinnings as exactly as our visible sinnings. “He knows the secrets of the heart” (Ps 44:21). Would not a criminal at the bar speak truly, if he knew, if he believed, that the judge had windows that looked into his breast?

Athenodorus, a heathen, could say that all men ought to be careful in the actions of their life, because God was everywhere and saw all that was done.

Zeno, a wise heathen, affirmed that God saw even the thoughts.

It was an excellent saying of Ambrose, “If you cannot hide yourself from the sun, which is God’s minister of light, how impossible it will be to hide yourself from Him Whose eyes are ten thousand times brighter than the sun!” Though a sinner may outwit his conscience, yet he cannot outwit the eye of God’s omniscience. Oh, that poor souls would remember that, as they are never out of the reach of God’s hand, so they are never out from under the view of His eye! God is #emph[totus oculus], “all eye.” “For My eyes are on all their ways. They are not hidden from My face, and their guilt is not concealed from My eyes” (Jer 16:17). “For His eyes are on the ways of a man, and He sees his every step. There is no darkness or deep shadow where the workers of iniquity can hide” (Job 34:21–22). “Your eyes are on all the ways of the sons of men, to reward each one according to his ways and according to the fruit of his deeds” (Jer 32:19).

You know what Ahasuerus, that great monarch, said concerning Haman, when he came in and found him thrown down on the couch on which the queen sat. “What?” he says, “Would he actually assault the queen … before me in the house?” (Esth 7:8). The deadly emphasis was in the words “before me.” “Would he assault the queen … #emph[before me]?” What, will he dare to commit such villainy while I stand and look on? Oh, sirs! To sin in the sight of God, to do wickedly under the eye of God, is a thing that He regards as the greatest affront and the highest indignity that can possibly be done to Him. What, He says, will you be drunk before Me? Will you swear and blaspheme before Me? Will you be wanton and unclean before Me? Will you be unjust and unrighteous under My eye? Will you profane My Sabbaths and pollute My ordinances before My face? Will you despise and persecute My servants in My presence?

This, then, is what gives all sin its deadly weight: it is done before the face of God, it is committed in the royal presence of the King of kings—whereas the very consideration of God’s omnipresence should nobly arm us against sin and Satan. The consideration of His all-seeing eye should make us shun all occasions of sin and make us shy of all appearances of sin. Shall the eye of the master keep the schoolboy from blotting his copybook? Shall the eye of the judge keep the criminal from pilfering and stealing? Shall the eye of the master keep the servant from idling and trifling? Shall the eye of the father keep the child from wandering and roaming? Shall the eye of the husband keep the wife from excesses and indecencies? Shall the sharp eye of wise Cato, or the keen eye of a near neighbor, or the severe eye of a bosom friend keep you from many enormities and vanities? And shall not the strict, the pure, the jealous eye of an all-seeing God keep you from sinning in the secret room, when all the curtains are drawn, the doors bolted, and everyone in the house in bed or away from home but you and your Delilah? Oh, what dreadful atheism is bound up in the heart of the man who is more afraid of the eye of his father, his pastor, his child, his servant, than he is of the eye, the presence, of the eternal God! Oh, that all whom this concerns would take such serious notice of it as to judge themselves severely for it, to mourn bitterly over it, and to strive mightily in prayer with God both for the pardon of it and for power against it!

The apostle sadly complains of some in his time who wallowed in secret sins. “For it is shameful even to mention what the disobedient do in secret” (Eph 5:12). He speaks of those who lived in secret fornication and uncleanness. There were many who had put on a form of godliness, who yet allowed themselves in the secret practice of abominable wickedness and filthiness, as if there were no God to see them, no conscience to accuse them, no Judgment Day to arraign them, no justice to condemn them, and no hell to torment them. Oh! How infinitely odious they must be in the eyes of a holy God, who can court and flatter Him highly in public, and yet are so bold as to provoke Him to His face in private! They are like those whores who make a great show of affection and respect for their husbands in public, and yet at home will play the harlot before their husbands’ eyes.

Some perform religious duties only to cloak and color over their secret filthiness, their secret wickedness; some pretend to pay their vows, and yet wait for the twilight (Prov 7:13–15; Job 24:15); some commit wickedness in a corner, and yet with the harlot wipe their mouths and say, “What have we done?” Such people will at last find the rooms, the stone out of the wall, the beam out of the timber, the seats they sit on, and the beds they lie on, witnessing against all their wanton caresses and lewd behavior in secret (Hab 2:11). “God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers” (Heb 13:4). He will sentence them Himself. And why, but because such sinners carry on so secretly and craftily that often no one but God can find them out? Magistrates often neglect to punish such sinners even when their secret wickedness is made known; and therefore God Himself will sit in judgment on them. Though they may escape the eyes of men, yet they will never escape the judgment of God. Iniquities of the heart do not come under any human sentence. The sexually immoral and adulterers are usually marvelously close, secretive, and cunning in concealing their abominable filthiness; therefore the harlot is said to be subtle of heart (Prov 7:10). Rabbi Solomon translates the Hebrew #emph[munito corde], “having her heart fenced.” “For,” he says, “as a city is surrounded with fortifications, so her heart is fortified all around with subtlety.” Or else it may be rendered #emph[occlusa corde], “fast shut up in the heart, as close as a besieged city,” that is, “most secret in the subtlety of her heart, however open she may be in the boldness of her outward behavior.” So the prophet Agur counts the way of a man with a maiden, and the way of an adulterous woman, among the things which neither he himself nor any other man could possibly trace and find out, and compares it to the way of three things which no mind or industry of man is able to make out. But yet God sees all, and will bring them to the bar for all (Prov 30:19–20).

==== 2. Secret Sins Shall Be Revealed

Secondly, consider that secret sins shall be revealed. The most hidden works of darkness shall be openly made manifest, for though sin is acted in the dark, yet sin shall be judged in the light. “For there is nothing secret that will not be disclosed, and nothing concealed that will not be made known and brought to light” (Luke 8:17). The slanders of the Jews about the magical arts of Christ and His apostles, and the horrible lies of the pagans about the incestuous unions of the Christians and their drinking of man’s blood, were in time exposed for what they were. “For God will bring every work into judgment, along with every secret thing, whether good or evil” (Eccl 12:14). Note, he does not say “some work,” but “every work”; and not only works, but secrets; and not only secrets, but every secret; and not only secret good things but evil too. Whether good works or ill works, whether secret or open, all must be brought to judgment. The books of God’s omniscience and of man’s conscience shall then be opened; and then secret sins shall be as legible on your forehead as if they were written with the most glittering sunbeams on a wall of crystal. All men’s secret sins are printed in heaven, and God will at last read them aloud in the ears of all the world. “Judge nothing before the appointed time; wait until the Lord comes. He will bring to light what is hidden in darkness and will expose the motives of men’s hearts” (1 Cor 4:5).

Just as there is a world of flies and specks of dust in the air which we never see until the sun shines, so there are many thousands upon thousands of proud thoughts, and unclean thoughts, and worldly thoughts, and malicious thoughts, and envious thoughts, and bloody thoughts, which the world does not see and does not know. But on the Great Day, when the motives of all hearts shall be exposed, then all shall come out, then all shall appear, both to the upper and the lower world. On that great day all masks, visors, and hoods shall be pulled off, and then all shall come out. All that you have ever done in the secret room, in the dark corner, shall be made known to men and angels, yes, to the whole court of heaven and to all the rest of the world. “On the day when God will judge men’s secrets through Christ Jesus” (Rom 2:16). On this Great Day, God will judge not only our words but our works, not only our open works but also our secret works and ways. When Jehoiakim was dead, the characters, superstitious marks, and prints of his sorcery were found on his body (2 Chr 36:8). This shows how deeply idolatry was rooted in his heart, seeing he bore its marks in his flesh during his life. Being a king, he carried it off boldly and kept everything hidden; but when he was dead, then all came out, then the marks of his abominable idolatry appeared on his body. Sinners, even the greatest of sinners, may hide and keep secret their horrid abominations for a time; yet a time will come when all shall come out, when all their secret marks and secret abominations shall be plain to all the world.

But sinners may be ready to object and say, “Only leave us alone in our secret sins until that day, and then we shall do well enough.” And therefore,

==== 3. God Many Times Reveals Secret Sins Even in This Life

In the third place, consider that God many times, even in this life, reveals and makes known to the world men’s secret sins (Isa 41:21–23).

God loves to act in keeping with His own names. Now, to be “a revealer of secrets” is one of His names (Dan 2:47); and accordingly, even in this world, He often brings to light the most hidden things of darkness.

Of all the glorious attributes of God, there is none that suffers so deeply by secret sins as the attribute of His omniscience. And therefore, in this world God often stands up to vindicate the honor of that attribute by unmasking sinners and by bringing to light all those secret paths and ways of wickedness in which they have long walked undetected. It was for the honor of this blessed attribute of God that the secretly plotted sin of Ananias and Sapphira was so openly revealed (Acts 5:1–12). “And great fear came over the whole church and all who heard about these events” (Acts 5:11).

Joseph’s brothers for a long time hid their malice, their craft, their cruelty, their envy, their treachery, in selling their brother into Egypt; but at last, by amazing and bewildering providences, all was brought to light (Gen 42:21–22; 50:15–22). Conscience, though for a time it may seem to be asleep, will yet in time awake and make the sinner know that it is as faithful in recording as it is terrible in accusing; and this Joseph’s brothers found by sad experience.

So Gehazi: he sins secretly, he lies fearfully, and after all he defends it stoutly. But at last all comes out, and instead of being clothed richly, he and his descendants were clothed with leprosy forever. And instead of two changes of garments, God hangs them up in chains as a monument of His wrath to all generations (2 Kgs 5:20ff).

So Achan secretly and sacrilegiously steals a fine Babylonian garment, and two hundred shekels of silver, and a wedge of gold of fifty shekels’ weight, and hides them in the earth in the middle of his tent; and because of this, Israel flees before their enemies. But at last Achan is taken, and all comes out, and his golden wedge proved a wedge to split him, and his Babylonian garment a garment to shroud him (Josh 7). Joshua makes a bonfire of all that he had secretly and sinfully stolen, and in it burns him and his children and all that he had. Oh, how openly, how severely, God sometimes punishes men for their most secret iniquity!

You may see the same in that great instance of David. “Why then have you despised the command of the #smallcaps[Lord] by doing evil in His sight? You put Uriah the Hittite to the sword (this was done in a secret letter) and took his wife as your own … Now, therefore, the sword will never depart from your house, because you have despised Me and have taken the wife of Uriah the Hittite to be your own. This is what the #smallcaps[Lord] says: ‘I will raise up adversity against you from your own house. Before your very eyes I will take your wives and give them to another, and he will lie with them in broad daylight. You have acted in secret, but I will do this thing in broad daylight before all Israel’” (2 Sam 12:9–12; 16:22). David was very intent and very industrious to hide his sin and to save his reputation, but the covering made of Uriah’s blood was too short and too narrow to hide his folly with Bathsheba. And therefore, when he had done all he could, his sin was tossed like a ball from man to man through court, city, and country.

I have read of one Parthenius, a murderer, treasurer to Theodobert king of France, who had treacherously slain a special friend of his called Ausanius, with his wife Papianilla. When no man suspected or accused him of it, he exposed and accused himself in this strange manner. As he slept in his bed, he suddenly roared out most pitifully, crying for help or else he would perish. And being asked what ailed him, he answered, half asleep, that his friend Ausanius and his wife, whom he had slain long ago, were summoning him to judgment before God. Upon this confession he was arrested and, after due examination, stoned to death. Thus the terrors and horrors of his own conscience revealed that secret wickedness which no one could prove against him.

I have read how Mahomet the great Turk, with great rewards, had hired two Turks to undertake to kill Scanderbeg. These traitors came to Scanderbeg making such a show of detesting both Mahomet’s tyrannical government and his vain superstition that they were held, both by Scanderbeg and by others, to be indeed the men they wished to be taken for. And so, after they had learned the principles of the Christian religion, they were both baptized at their own desire. Soon after, by a providence, it so happened that these two traitors fell out with each other, by which means the plot came to be revealed; and after due examination and confession of the deed, they were at once condemned and executed.

Conscience is God’s spy in the bosom. Conscience, as a scribe, a registrar, sits in the closet of your#super[+] hearts with pen in hand and keeps a daily record of all your secret ways and secret crimes, which are beyond the knowledge of men. Conscience sets down the time when, the place where, the manner how, and the persons with whom such and such secret wickednesses have been committed—and that so clearly and plainly that, go where you#super[+] will and do what you can, the record of them shall never be cancelled or erased until God appears in judgment.

Let a man sin in the most secret seclusion that human cunning can contrive; let him take every way he can to hide his sins, to cloak and cover his sin, as Adam did; yet conscience will so play the judge that it will bring in the evidence, produce the law, press the penalty, and pass the sentence of condemnation upon him. There is many a man who makes a fair profession of faith and has a great name in the world who is yet αὐτοκατάκριτος, self-condemned for those secret sins which are neither plain to the eyes of man nor punishable by the laws or hands of men. Indeed, many times in this life God raises such a hell of horror and terror in many men’s consciences because of their secret sins that they can have no rest or quiet, neither in bed nor at table, neither lying down nor rising up. They would gladly conceal their sins; they are unwilling that the world should know how vile they have been in secret. But with conscience on the rack, and always gnawing, accusing, and condemning them, they can hold out no longer. Now all must come out, and now those sins that were most secret and concealed come to be proclaimed on the housetop.

Some who have been in anguish of conscience, others who have been struck with delirium, and many in their very sleep, have often been the ones to spread abroad and proclaim their own secret filthiness and wickedness. In such cases, God has made many a secret sinner cry out with the leper, “Unclean, unclean!” (Lev 13:45), and with Judas before all present, “I have sinned, I have sinned” (Matt 27:4). Many times in this life God very strangely and wonderfully reveals those secret works of darkness in which people have long lived undetected.

A Pythagorean bought a pair of shoes on credit. The shoemaker dies; he is glad, and thinks the shoes his for nothing. But a while after, his conscience flies at him and becomes a continual scold and tormentor to him. At this he goes to the dead man’s house and throws in his money with these words: “There, take your due; you live to me, though dead to all others.” But,

==== 4. Secret Sins Are in Some Respects More Dangerous Than Open Sins

Fourthly, consider that secret sins are in some respects more dangerous than open sins. Many a man bleeds to death inwardly, and no man perceives it. The more inward and secret the disease is, the more the man is in danger of losing his life. There are no fevers so dangerous as those that prey on the vital spirits and inward parts; so there are no sins so dangerous and destructive to the souls of men as those that are most inward and secret. Secret sins often reign in the souls of men most powerfully when least visibly.

First, consider that he who sins secretly deprives himself of those helps and remedies which, by a divine blessing, might arm him against sin, indeed, make him victorious over sin: namely, the prayers, counsels, rebukes, examples, and encouragements of friends and relatives. A man’s house may be on fire, but while it is all inside, no help comes in. But when the fire flames out, when it catches the outside of the house, then help runs in; then help is ready on every hand. He who sins in secret shuts himself off from all public remedy, and takes great pains to damn his soul in a corner and to go to hell in the dark.

Secondly, secret sins will make way for public sins. He who has no scruple about sinning in the secret room will before long, with Absalom, be ready to spread a tent on the top of the house and to go in to his concubines in the sight of all Israel (2 Sam 16:21–22). Those who have had no scruple about stealing a few pins or pennies or a few shillings in private have in time come to be so bold as to take a purse on the highway at high noon. The cockatrice must be crushed in the egg, or it will soon become a serpent. The very thought of sin, if not looked to, will break out into action, action into custom, custom into habit, and then both body and soul are irrecoverably lost to all eternity.

If Satan can only wound our heel, as the poets imagine of Achilles, he will go to great lengths to send death from the heel to the heart. If this subtle serpent can only wriggle in his tail by an evil thought, he will soon get in his head by a worse action. That is why Christ calls hatred murder, and a lustful eye adultery. Secret hatred often ends in open murder, and secret lustful glances of the eye often end in visible adultery. If Amnon is sick with the sinful conceptions of incestuous lust, how his soul will be in pain and labor until he has brought forth! And how many there are who in secret have taken now and then just one cup more than enough, who now may be seen at high noon reeling against every post! Just as secret diseases in the body, if not cured, will in time openly break out, so secret sins in the soul, if not pardoned and purged, will in time be openly revealed. Covetousness was Judas’s secret sin; and no sooner does an occasion or a temptation present itself than he is very ready and eager to betray and sell his Lord and Master for thirty pieces of silver before all the world. “After desire has conceived, it gives birth to sin” (Jas 1:15)—and that in this way:

First, sin has its conception, and that is delight.

And then its formation, and that is design.

And then its birth, and that is action.

And then its growth, and that is custom.

And then its end, and that is damnation.

Thirdly, secret sinning shows far more respect and fear toward men than toward God. You will be unjust in secret, and lustful in secret, and unclean in secret, and treacherous in secret; and why, but because you are afraid that such or such men should know it, or that such and such friends should know it, or that such and such relatives should know it? Ah, poor wretch, are you afraid of the eye of a man, “of mortal man, of a son of man who withers like grass” (Isa 51:12)? And yet do you not tremble under the eye of Him whose eyes are “like a blazing fire,” sharp and terrible, such as pierce into the inward parts (Rev 1:14; Heb 4:12–13)? Ah, how full of atheism is the heart of that man who silently says, “If only my sins are hidden from the eyes of the world, I do not care though the Lord knows them, though the Lord strictly observes them, though the Lord sets a mark, a memorandum, on them.” What is this, O man, but to defy God, and to tempt Him, and to provoke Him to His very face, who “is light, and in Him there is no darkness at all” (1 John 1:5)? Ah, sinner, sinner, can man damn you? Can man disinherit you? Can man fill your conscience with horrors and terrors? Can man make your life a very hell? Can man bar the gates of glory against you? Can man speak you into the grave by a word of his mouth? And after all, can man cast you into endless, easeless, and remediless torments? Oh, no! Can God do all this? Oh, yes! Why, then, does your heart not stand more in awe of the eye of the great God than of the eye of a poor, weak, mortal man?

I have dwelt the longer on this particular point because there is not any one thing in all the world that more hinders secret communion with God and secret prayer than secret sins. And oh, that you#super[+] would all make it your great business to watch against secret sins, and to pray against secret sins, and to mourn over secret sins, and deeply to judge and condemn yourselves for secret sins, and carefully and conscientiously to shun and avoid all occasions and provocations that may be as fuel to secret sins!

Certainly, there are no men or women so sincere and serious in closet prayer, or so frequent, so fervent, so constant in closet prayer, or so delighted, so resolute, so undaunted, or so unwearied in closet prayer, as those who keep themselves most clear and free from secret sins.

To close, remember this: that though secret sins are in some respects more dangerous than other sins, yet in three respects they are not so bad nor so dangerous as other sins.

First, in that they do not bring such scandal on religion as open sins do.

Secondly, in that they do not shame, grieve, and wound the hearts of the saints as open sins do.

Thirdly, in that they are not so infectious to others, nor such provocations to others to sin against the Lord, as open sins are.

And thus you#super[+] may see what those things are that you must carefully take heed of, if ever you would devote yourselves to closet prayer.
